::ingredients

(whey, for whey-based version) 2l
(milk) 500ml with whey; 2l if using milk only
(table salt) 10g
(acid) = (citric acid) 6g -OR- (lemon juice) 130ml

::equipment

(thermometer)
(cheesecloth) 1
(cheese recipient) 1
(pot)
(fridge)

::instructions

[
    (whey) 2l + (milk) 500ml
        <pour, into (pot)>
]
-OR-
[
    (milk) 2l <pour, into (pot)>
]
(pot) + (table salt)
{mixture} <stir, well>
    <heat, to 80°C, using (thermometer)>
    + (acid)
    <stir, well>
# You should already see the acid reacting with the milk or whey.
{mixture} <heat, to 90°C>
{ricotta} <pour, into (cheesecloth)>
Let the whey drip out.
{ricotta} <move, into (cheese recipient)>
    <store, in (fridge)>

::notes

The ricotta is an Italian cheese made by cooking the whey (liquid byproduct of the cheese production), by cooking it at an high temperature (80-90°C), hence the name "ricotta", which means "cooked again". Even though it's typically made from the whey, you can make it directly from the milk as well.


